INT. DINING CAR - OVERNIGHT EXPRESS - NIGHT

White linen, silver pots, amber lamps. The train sways. Dark country slides past the windows.

ELEANOR WHITCOMBE, fifties, in a grey travelling suit, sits alone at a table for two, a cup of black coffee cooling in front of her. Across the car, AUGUSTIN ROCHE, sixties, a gaunt gentleman in a dinner jacket, folds his newspaper and approaches.

ROCHE
Madame Whitcombe.

ELEANOR
Monsieur Roche.

ROCHE
I did not expect to find you on this train.

ELEANOR
I did not expect to be found.

He takes the chair opposite without being asked. She lets him.

ROCHE
Twenty years.

ELEANOR
Twenty-two.

ROCHE
You haven't changed.

ELEANOR
You have, thank God.

He smiles. A PORTER in a white jacket appears at the table's edge with a silver pot.

PORTER
More coffee, madame?

ELEANOR
No. Thank you.

ROCHE
Nothing for me.

The Porter withdraws. The train rocks into a curve.

ROCHE (CONT'D)
I kept the letters, you know.

ELEANOR
I burned yours.

ROCHE
I assumed.

ELEANOR
Do not make it sound like a kindness.

ROCHE
It was not meant as one.

She lifts her cup. Sets it down. The lamp flickers.
